+# By-ID strength projection and prepared similarity
+
+This child uses query-strength `135b06a` (including canonical schema `4391118`), namespace-batch `0e58f3b`, and the validated minimal similarity parent `98179a4`. The latter contains only its actual scoped-read, vector-normalization and graph-admission prerequisites (#37/#28/#39), not the broad integration branch. The required storage parent is core45 `1ca36c4`, including disposable pending-plan preflight, removal-ledger publication and terminal UUID protection. The actual composition parent is `8c39de1`. A constructor-only backport is insufficient for the known pending-plan issue; the full required core parent supplies the validated fix. Ordinary serving guards and schema rejection remain intact. Namespace enumeration stays `namespace_records`, with query projection before eligibility filtering.
+
+`QueryEngine::get_memory_with_access` and the shared similarity helper resolve the canonical source's namespace through `NamespaceResolver::resolve_id`. They never substitute cached source metadata or guess the connection default. One `StrengthContext` retains the original requested access flag, namespace policy/revision and one clock sample. Explicit-policy operations project the namespace's canonical live donors before projecting source/results, using the existing graph bonus calculation without query tag filters. Returned raw/effective strengths change with the clock; committed phase, timestamps, retention and cosine scores/order do not. Legacy-policy strengths remain canonical materialized values, including for schema-v2 records with the legacy lifecycle. Legacy operations do not enumerate donor records.
+
+The Rust-only `prepare_similar_filtered(id, limit, min_score, requested_access)` shares selection with `similar_filtered_with_access`; a separate private `defer_recording` flag postpones exposure. The API `SearchPipeline` trait and production adapter expose the same prepared helper. REST passes the original requested access flag, then hydrates canonical metadata and full text, checks its original admission stamp and invokes the existing late recording hook only for true mode. It overlays only explicit-policy raw/effective strengths from the prepared result; legacy canonical difficulty/strength fields remain untouched. MCP similarity uses the ordinary filtered helper with the original flag. No transport-v2 mode or new response envelope is exposed.
+
+Explicit-policy true mode still fails closed before vector selection, including zero-limit or empty-threshold selections, and before sampling the projection clock. The late similarity hook also rejects explicit-policy records on canonical preflight without repinning a clock/policy. That preflight is not a transaction guard: the later A3 writer join must reject unsupported lifecycle policy under the metadata publication guard, using the canonical namespace decoder. Future deferred general-search/MCP-recall consumers must preserve original intent through a corresponding prepared-search path; calling an internal false-mode search and later recording is not valid policy admission.
+
+The existing get/similar and transport admission checks, source-vector normalization, fallible full-text hydration and awaited/deduplicated legacy similarity recorder are retained. Direct-fetch projection here is the QueryEngine helper; standalone REST/MCP direct/batch hydration consumers remain a separate integration task. Legacy direct-helper recording remains its existing asynchronous behavior pending the observation-writer join.
+
+Tests use strict, owned `OfflineSnapshot` reads of isolated migrated fixtures, never ordinary schema-v2 serving open. Test-only HTTP storage reads canonical records/payloads through that snapshot; the actual REST router, API search adapter, MCP dispatcher and MCP search adapter exercise production selection. Placeholder legacy subsystems have no access to personal data or a running daemon. Fixtures retain unknown legacy embedding provenance. False-mode tests compare canonical file bytes and use record/cache traps. Six new tests cover scope, wrong-scope/missing-lifecycle/future-revision rejection, projected donors and strengths, clock advancement without retention changes, cosine stability, legacy parity, original-intent preservation and actual REST/MCP behavior. Parent regressions continue to cover namespace assertions, vector normalization and admission drift.
+
+Validation: the core45 parallel run passed 711 library tests and failed one inherited `effects_crash_reopen_replays_before_cleanup_once_logically` test with `DatabaseLocked` at engine open. The log is retained as `.validation/all-tests-core45-parallel.log`; the concurrent ownership-lock issue remains unresolved, with no cause inferred here. All six new by-ID tests, the malformed-pending-plan byte-preservation test, and terminal UUID regressions passed in that run. Final serial validation on source `6f29a74` passed **712 library tests plus one CLI integration test (713 total)** using `cargo test --locked --offline --all-targets --features bench -- --test-threads=1`. Formatting and all-target Clippy passed (with existing warning classes). This serial pass does not resolve or conceal the parallel ownership-lock failure; no serialization or retry change was added to source/tests. Logs: `.validation/all-tests.log`, `fmt-check.log`, and `clippy.log`. Commands use `recalld-ci:1.94`, `RUSTUP_TOOLCHAIN=1.94.1`, `--network none --cpus 2 --memory 4g`, two Cargo jobs and private `.validation/cargo` / `.validation/target`. No home, credentials or Docker socket is mounted. Logs and caches are untracked.
+
+Remaining gates: schema-aware transactional observation writer and prepared general-search join; direct/batch transport projection; A1 generation-consistent serving/readiness and all-writer participation; lifecycle policy/feedback writer integration; full v2 envelopes and A5/A6 measurements. This slice does not activate serving, bless an unknown embedding profile, claim coherent v2 operation or promote performance. Explicit donor enumeration cost still needs measurement; no timing field is added.
+
+## Separate structured-label assessment (not implemented)
+
+The next minimal mapping fix belongs at `CachedRecord::from(&DiskRecord)`: schema-present `StructuredLabels.entities` is authoritative, including an empty list, and must be cloned without tag parsing, normalization, truncation or UTF-8 reconstruction. Only schema-absent records use the legacy tag fallback. This was coordinated with the schema/structured-label owners. It needs long/non-ASCII labels, authoritative-empty-vs-stale-tag and exact round-trip regressions, followed by entity-index consumers using their existing normalized matching boundary. No entity-label or catalog writer change is bundled here.
